1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,350
1975.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,150
A young engineer at Kodak
builds a strange gadget:

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,670
a toaster-sized box with a lens, a
tape recorder, and a tiny screen.

4
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:12,190
It's the world's first digital camera.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:17,870
His bosses look at it and say: "That's
cute, but don't tell anyone about it."

6
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:21,550
37 years later, Kodak
files for bankruptcy.

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:25,870
George Eastman founded Kodak in the
late 1800s with a simple slogan:

8
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:30,470
"You press the button, we do the rest."
Photography became something for

9
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:31,830
everyone.

10
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:34,550
For about a century, Kodak dominated.

11
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:39,310
In the United States it controlled the
vast majority of film and camera sales.

12
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,050
At its peak in the 1980s it
employed well over 100,000 people.

13
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,670
Kodak made money the way
a razor company does:

14
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:51,470
sell the camera cheaply, then
earn forever on the film.

15
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:57,030
In 1975, Steve Sasson, a Kodak engineer,
built the first self-contained digital

16
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:58,230
camera.

17
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:04,110
It captured a 0.01-megapixel black-and-white
image, and took about 23 seconds to

18
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:06,830
record it onto a cassette tape.

19
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:10,590
Sasson later recalled that management's
reaction was less excitement and more

20
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,270
confusion.

21
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:15,510
Why would anyone want to look
at pictures on a TV screen?

22
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:20,470
And, more importantly, it threatened
Kodak's most profitable business:

23
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:21,370
film.

24
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:25,950
Kodak did patent the technology, and
even sold early digital cameras in the

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,550
1990s.

26
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:32,430
But the leadership kept protecting
film, which was still printing money.

27
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:34,510
This is the classic innovator's dilemma.

28
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,270
Digital photography would be cheaper,
but it would also destroy the film

29
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:39,910
revenue.

30
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,910
Every step toward digital felt like
cutting off the branch the company was

31
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:44,270
sitting on.

32
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:45,910
So Kodak hesitated.

33
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,470
Competitors didn't.

34
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:53,280
Japanese companies like Sony, Canon
and Fujifilm pushed digital hard.

35
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:58,710
Fujifilm, Kodak's rival in film, used
its chemistry know-how to expand into

36
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:02,430
other industries, such as
healthcare and cosmetics.

37
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:06,110
Kodak stayed too attached
to photos and film.

38
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,990
Then came phones.

39
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:13,070
By the 2000s, cameras were built into
mobile phones, and sharing photos online

40
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:15,150
replaced printing them.

41
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:19,230
Kodak's film sales fell
sharply, year after year.

42
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:24,350
In January 2012, Kodak filed for
Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection.

43
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:27,010
The cruelest comparison
came that same year.

44
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:32,470
Facebook bought Instagram, a photo-sharing
app with just 13 employees, for about

45
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,190
$1 billion.

46
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:39,270
Kodak, the company that once defined
photography, had once employed over 100,000

47
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:40,110
people.

48
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:44,630
Kodak emerged from bankruptcy in 2013
as a much smaller company, focused on

49
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:49,230
commercial printing and imaging,
and sold off many of its patents.

50
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,430
So what are the lessons?

51
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:52,190
One.

52
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:54,070
Having the invention isn't enough.

53
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:56,590
Kodak had the technology first.

54
00:02:56,640 --> 00:02:59,630
What mattered was whether
it was willing to commit.

55
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:00,470
Two.

56
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:02,830
Protecting your cash cow can be fatal.

57
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,990
If you don't cannibalize your
own product, someone else will.

58
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:08,430
Three.

59
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,190
Know what business you're in.

60
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:12,350
Kodak thought it was in the film business.

61
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:15,970
It was really in the business of
capturing and sharing memories.

62
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:19,510
Smartphones and social
media did that better.

63
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:21,910
Kodak didn't miss the future.

64
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:26,670
It saw it, built it... and
then decided it wasn't ready.

65
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:30,790
In business, sometimes the
biggest risk is doing nothing.

66
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:35,510
Next video: the phone company that
once owned 40% of the world...

67
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:37,180
and lost it all in a few years.
